<section class="page-head">
  <h1>Where RAG stops and Code starts</h1>
  <p class="section-note">A plain-English question box over a pile of messy AP data, and what it taught me about where retrieval earns its keep and where it quietly gets things wrong.</p>
</section>

<section class="case-study">
  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Every team sitting on a pile of documents wants the same thing: ask a question in plain English
    and get the answer. The default way to build that today is RAG, retrieval-augmented generation,
    where a model searches your documents and answers from what it finds. I wanted to see where that
    actually holds up, so I pointed it at the data from my
    <a href="/posts/ai-evals/">invoice-reconciliation project</a>: a few dozen invoices, their
    purchase orders, and a bank statement.
  </p>

  <h2>How I framed it</h2>
  <p>
    Some questions are a look-up. "What's the total on invoice 19296" lives on one document, and
    retrieval is good at finding that document. Other questions are a computation. "How much did we
    pay Marigold in total," or "how many invoices are still unpaid," need every matching row, counted
    or added, with nothing missed. Those are two different jobs, so I built a router that reads the
    question first and sends it the right way. Look-ups go to retrieval. Counts, totals, and lists go
    to a query over the reconciled ledger, which is exact and shows the rows it used. Same instinct as
    <a href="/posts/agent-vs-code/">an earlier post</a>: let the model do the messy reading, let code
    own the numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>The build</h2>
  <p>
    A searchable chunk for every invoice and PO. A plain retriever. A router that classifies intent.
    A set of structured queries over the ledger. And, because a claim like this is worth checking, a
    16-question eval with known answers and a scorecard. The whole thing runs in your browser with no
    server, which is why you can try it below.
  </p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <img src="/img/rag-architecture.svg" alt="Question-answering architecture. A plain-English question goes to a router that classifies intent. Look-up questions take the retrieval lane: search the chunks by similarity and answer from the top matches, bounded by what retrieval surfaced. Count, total, and list questions take the code lane: query the reconciled ledger across every row and return an exact answer with the rows it used. Both lanes meet at a single answer." />
    <figcaption>The router is the whole trick. Look-up questions take the retrieval lane. Count, total, and list questions take the code lane, which reads every row and returns the rows it used. When the router can't place a question, it asks for a person instead of guessing.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Try it</h2>
  <p>
    Ask your own question, or tap an example. The left panel is retrieval only. The right is routed to
    code, verified against the ledger. They agree on look-ups and split on anything that needs the
    whole ledger.
  </p>

